      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Five cases are described that offer evidence for the existence of the \"serotonin irritation syndrome,\" an anxiety state associated with high cation environments, elevated serum serotonin, and decreased urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid. The therapeutic response and reduction in serum serotonin after treatment with methysergide and/or removal from the high-cation environment support a serotonergic basis of the anxiety."},
